---
/**
 * The site header. Three states, one DOM, no media query.
 *
 *   STACKED  base    identity centered; nav below it on a full-bleed accent
 *                    band, in two rows of three
 *   ROW      md      the same, with the nav on ONE row — the state the Figma
 *                    boards never drew, and the reason it needed no design
 *   INLINE   lg      identity left, nav right, no band
 *
 * Each state is additive on the one before it, read off a `--breakpoint-*` flag
 * with a style container query. Nothing here undoes anything, which is what makes
 * the base state what a browser falls back to — see tokens.css → THE BREAKPOINTS.
 *
 * ── WHY THERE IS NO <Band> HERE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The masthead is chrome, and chrome is inset from the VIEWPORT rather than laid
 * on the content grid — the Figma boards put it 48px from each edge on Desktop
 * and 16 on Mobile, which is `chrome-inset` exactly, not `grid-margin`. So no
 * <Grid>. And <Band> would only contribute `data-space="none"` and a tone this
 * header does not have, while adding a wrapper between <header> and the two
 * things that have to be flex siblings in the inline state. So no <Band> either.
 *
 * That leaves the question the accent band poses: how does the nav reach the
 * viewport edge when the header is inset? Answer — WHICH ELEMENT CARRIES THE
 * INSET IS ITSELF PART OF THE STATE:
 *
 *   stacked  the two CHILDREN are inset; <header> is not, so the band bleeds
 *   inline   <header> is inset; the children are not, because there is no band
 *
 * Putting it on the children in both states is the obvious-looking version and it
 * is wrong. The identity's trailing inset and the nav's leading one look free —
 * one is behind a centered block, the other behind a nav pushed to the far side —
 * but they still occupy 96px of the header's MINIMUM width, which is enough to
 * make the inline layout overflow at its own breakpoint. It did: flex-shrink then
 * squeezed the identity until the wordmark broke onto two lines, and the header
 * came out 184px tall against the board's 144. Measured, not theorized.
 *
 * ── AND WHY THE BAND IS NOT A <Band> EITHER ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * <Band tone="accent"> re-points the whole role bundle, which is the right idea
 * and the wrong direction here: those declarations would have to be UNDONE at
 * lg, and restoring an inherited custom property from inside the block that
 * overrode it means `unset` and a paragraph explaining it. Three explicit
 * declarations per state is smaller than that, and reads in both directions.
 *
 * ── THE NAV / SEARCH SLOT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `.slot` is a seam, not decoration. Search expands IN PLACE, replacing the nav
 * items (DESIGN.md → Components → Search), so the two occupy one slot and the
 * slot has to be able to hold either. Phase 3 puts the nav in it and leaves the
 * toggle inert.
 */
import Logo from './Logo.astro'
import {Search} from '@lucide/astro'
---

{
  /*
  DOM ORDER IS READING ORDER at both widths, and nothing here depends on CSS to
  be legible: identity, then a list of links. That is the stacked configuration,
  so a document that loses its stylesheet entirely degrades to it rather than to
  a half-collapsed inline header.

  The wordmark and role are hardcoded, unlike the nav, and for a weaker reason —
  the nav is site structure by decision, but these two strings ARE content: the
  live site reads them from the Sanity `settings` singleton, where they currently
  say "Andy Fitzgerald Consulting" / "Content Operations Strategy & Design"
  against the board's shorter pair. Worth a decision before phase 4.
*/
}
<header class="masthead">
  <a class="identity" href="/" rel="home">
    <Logo class="mark" />
    <span class="wordmark">
      <span class="name">Andy Fitzgerald</span>
      <span class="role">Information Architect</span>
    </span>
  </a>

  <div class="slot">
    <nav aria-label="Main">
      {
        /*
        `role="list"` because Safari drops list semantics from a `list-style: none`
        list, which is the one thing that makes the declaration an a11y change
        rather than a cosmetic one.
      */
      }
      <ul role="list">
        <li><a href="/insights/">Insights</a></li>
        <li><a href="/projects/">Projects</a></li>
        <li><a href="/presentations/">Presentations</a></li>
        <li>
          {
            /*
            One link, one href, two labels — the terse one for the band and the
            verbose one for the inline nav, exactly parallel to the search item
            below. Two <li>s would mean two links to one page in one nav.

            THE SPACE BELONGS INSIDE `.label-inline`, and that is deliberate. With
            no space at all, a document that lost its stylesheet would read
            "ConsultingWork with me"; with the space outside the spans, the
            surviving label would carry a stray one and a centered label would sit
            half a space off. Inside the span it is removed with the span when the
            span is hidden, and when the OTHER span is hidden it lands first in the
            line box, where white-space processing drops it. Correct in all three
            cases, which is why it is written this way rather than tidily.

            It has to be `{' '}` rather than typed whitespace: Astro trims leading
            whitespace inside an element written across a line break, so a literal
            space here silently disappears from the build. Verified in the output,
            after writing it the other way first.
          */
          }
          <a href="/consulting/">
            <span class="label-stacked">Consulting</span>
            <span class="label-inline">{' '}Work with me</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li><a href="/contact/">Contact</a></li>
        <li>
          {
            /*
            INERT: no handler, wired in phase 4. A <button> rather than a link
            because search does not navigate — it replaces this slot's contents in
            place — so the element that gets behavior later is the element here now.

            The label is real text at BOTH widths, visually hidden rather than
            removed in the inline state. That is what lets an icon-only control
            have an accessible name without `aria-label`: Lucide marks its own SVG
            `aria-hidden`, so with `display: none` on the label the button would
            have no name at all.
          */
          }
          <button class="search-toggle" type="button">
            <Search class="search-icon" />
            <span class="search-label">Search</span>
          </button>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  /* ── The header ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* No padding, no width, no background. Stacked is plain block flow, so the
     stacked state needs no rule at all — see the note above about why the inset
     lives on the children. */

  /* ── Identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .identity {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-block: var(--chrome-pad-header);
    padding-inline: var(--chrome-inset);
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The mark names its role here; the component itself binds no color. */
  .mark {
    color: var(--color-accent);

    /* A flex item shrinks by default, and an SVG has no intrinsic minimum to stop
       it, so under any width pressure the disc goes oval before the text beside it
       gives an inch. base.css does this for Lucide icons for the same reason. */
    flex: none;
  }

  /* Name over role with NO gap — the boards stack them on their own line boxes
     and the leading does the spacing, which is why the block measures 62px at
     both widths despite the name changing size. */
  .name,
  .role {
    display: block;

    /* Stated on the children rather than on the link, so base.css's
       `a:hover { color: var(--color-link-hover) }` cannot reach them: it sets
       `color` on the <a>, and a child with its own value simply does not inherit
       it. Overriding it on `.identity:hover` instead would need (0,2,0) to beat
       the pseudo-class's (0,1,1) — a specificity race for no reason.

       DESIGN.md assigns no color role to either. `text` is the reading, since the
       wordmark is the site's own name rather than apparatus around content. */
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .name {
    /*
     * Noto Serif 400 — DESIGN.md's `masthead-name` role, kept here as a RULE
     * rather than a token because a semantic text role is a bundle of applied
     * properties. It briefly lived in tokens.css as `--role-masthead-name`, the
     * only `--role-*` token in the system, purely because its size steps by
     * viewport; reading `--breakpoint-lg` here does that without the exception.
     *
     * THE ONE ROLE WHOSE SIZE GROWS AS THE VIEWPORT NARROWS: 30px stacked,
     * 28.125 inline. A STEP at lg rather than an inverted clamp, because lg is
     * where the header restacks — a 2px snap inside a relayout that moves the
     * whole identity block is invisible, where the same snap drifting on its own
     * would not be.
     *
     * STATIC AT BOTH ENDS, and these two literals are the only place in the CSS
     * that retypes a value off the scale. They are `size/5`'s mobile endpoint and
     * `size/4`'s desktop endpoint — the ramp's ends, which are not themselves
     * tokens; only the clamps between them are. Using var(--font-size-5) and
     * var(--font-size-4) would ride those clamps and make the snap WORSE:
     * font-size-4 computes to 26.6px at lg, so the step would be 3.4px rather
     * than 1.9. The wordmark sits deliberately outside the type hierarchy, so
     * holding still at each end is the point. If the scale moves, move these.
     */
    font-family: var(--font-prose);
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .role {
    /* Lato 400 at size/3, riding the ordinary fluid ramp — only the name is
       special. */
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-3);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(
      --color-text-muted
    ); /* Muting to decrease relative prominence — this is too similar in size and color to .name */
  }

  /* NO HOVER TREATMENT ON THE IDENTITY, deliberately. An underline on the
     wordmark was tried and read as noise, and a color change is louder still —
     the identity is chrome, not a link in prose. A wordmark linking home is a
     strong enough convention that the pointer carries it. Nothing is lost for
     keyboard users either: the focus ring is unaffected. If it ever needs more,
     it wants a designed interaction rather than a borrowed link style. */

  /* ── The slot: a full-bleed accent band in the stacked state ────────────── */
  /*
   * Three declarations, not a token re-point. `color` is enough for everything
   * inside because base.css gives links `color: inherit` and Lucide strokes with
   * `currentColor`, so the band's text and its icons both follow from one value.
   */

  .slot {
    background-color: var(--color-surface-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-on-accent);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    padding-inline: var(--chrome-inset);
  }

  /* The focus ring needs NO override on the band, which is worth stating because
     it looks like it should. base.css orders the rings offset-then-ring, so on
     this band the inner 2px is `focus-offset` — white on light, near-black on
     dark, both legible against the fill — and the outer 2px is `focus-ring`,
     which blends into it. A tight legible ring rather than an offset one, in both
     themes. Verified against both dark values, not assumed. */

  /* ── Nav ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  ul {
    /* base.css's reset zeroes list margin but deliberately keeps the UA's
       padding-inline-start as a prose indent, so this list has to drop it. */
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    /*
     * ── EXPLICIT COLUMNS, NOT WRAPPING ──────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * A wrapping row would also produce the three states, but it would choose
     * the break points itself — 3+3, then 4+2, then 5+1 — and a lone "Search" on
     * its own row is not a layout anyone drew. Stating the column count instead
     * means the shape only ever changes where we say it does.
     *
     * No column gap, and that is not an omission. The board centers each label
     * in an equal share of the line, and every label is narrower than its share,
     * so the whitespace between hit areas is real without a gap declaration —
     * which also means the columns stay EVEN. A column gap would reintroduce the
     * unevenness this grid exists to prevent: `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, so a
     * track cannot shrink below its own min-content, and once the gaps take
     * enough room "Presentations" keeps its 107px while the other five share
     * what is left. That is exactly what happens at md — see the note there.
     */
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    row-gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  li {
    text-align: center;

    /* Inert at THIS state — grid ignores `flex` — and inert at lg, which
       overrides it. It is here for md alone, where the list becomes a flex row
       and the items need a shared basis to share the line. Leaving it in the
       base rule rather than the md block keeps one declaration instead of two,
       at the cost of looking dead where it is written. */
    flex: 1 1 6.5rem;

    /* base.css puts `margin-top: var(--rhythm-item)` on `li + li` for prose
       lists, which would land on items 2–6 here — including the ones already on
       the first row. `row-gap` above does the job correctly. */
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /*
   * base.css draws the wipe-in underline in `--color-link-hover`, which is
   * blue-500 — the band's own fill, so it would be invisible here. This is the
   * band treatment's fourth declaration, alongside the fill and the two text
   * colors above, and like them it is stated per state rather than re-pointed:
   * re-pointing the token would have to be undone at lg, and undoing an inherited
   * custom property from inside the block that overrode it needs `unset` and a
   * paragraph explaining it.
   *
   * No hover COLOR change. The underline carries it alone, which is both quieter
   * and what the live site does.
   */
  .slot :is(a, button)::after {
    border-block-end-color: var(--color-text-on-accent-hover);
  }

  /* ── The two swapped labels ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Each pair keeps the STACKED member in normal flow and hides its inline twin,
     so the base state is the one a document keeps if the container query below
     never evaluates. */

  .label-inline {
    display: none;
  }

  .search-icon {
    display: none;
  }

  .search-toggle {
    /* Not `all: unset` — that would take the font, cursor and inherited color
       with it. Just the five things a UA button brings that this one does not
       want. */
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    cursor: pointer;

    /*
     * `flex`, NOT `inline-flex` — this is the fix for the icon sitting high
     * against the other nav items.
     *
     * Once the label is visually hidden the button's only in-flow child is the
     * 20px icon, so the button collapses to 20px. As an INLINE-level box it then
     * participates in the row's inline formatting context, and a flex container's
     * baseline with no baseline-aligned items is synthesized from its bottom
     * margin edge — so the icon's bottom landed on the text baseline and the
     * whole glyph sat above it, about 3px high.
     *
     * Going block-level takes it out of that inline context entirely, and
     * `1lh` gives it the nav's own line height, so it matches the text rows it
     * sits beside without a number being typed. Measured against the "Contact"
     * row: 0.0px off center, and the Figma board agrees (20px icon at y=3.5 in a
     * 27px row).
     */
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-block-size: 1lh;

    /* A <button> sizes to fit-content whatever its `display` is, so going
       block-level above stopped it inheriting the cell's `text-align: center` and
       left "Search" flush left in its column while every other label stayed
       centered. Auto margins center the box itself.
       Deliberately not `width: 100%`: that would center the word too, but it
       would also stretch the focus ring across the whole cell, where every
       neighboring link's ring hugs its text. */
    margin-inline: auto;

    /* Containing block for the visually-hidden label in the inline state, so it
       cannot land somewhere that lengthens the page. */
    position: relative;
  }

  /* base.css hangs the nav underline 0.15em below its host box. That lands right
     for the links, which are INLINE and whose boxes stop at the descent line —
     but this button is block-level and fills the whole row, so its box bottom is
     already a half-leading lower and the rule came out 2.5px below its
     neighbours'. Anchoring it flush to its own bottom edge puts it back within
     0.2px of theirs. The half-leading itself is not derivable — it needs the
     font's content height — so this pins the box rather than computing the gap. */
  .search-toggle::after {
    inset-block-end: 0;
  }

  /* ── Row: md ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * Still on the band, still centered, but the six items go to one row. This is
   * the tablet state the boards never drew.
   *
   * ONE KNOWN ROUGH EDGE, unresolved rather than overlooked: between 768 and
   * about 864 the items are NOT even — 89,89,107,89,89,89 at 768 — because the
   * 24px gaps take enough of the line that "Presentations" falls back on its own
   * min-content while the other five share the rest. Flex and grid behave
   * identically here; `flex: 1 1 6.5rem` and `1fr` have the same min-content
   * floor, so switching between them changes nothing.
   *
   * The one-line alternative is `grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr)` with no
   * column gap, keeping the base state's mechanism and giving an even 112px per
   * column at 768. It reads as the more consistent answer; it is not made here
   * because it is a layout call, not a comment fix.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    ul {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
      column-gap: var(--space-3);
    }
  }

  /* ── Inline: lg ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * The header stops being a stack and becomes one row: identity left, nav
   * right, band gone. The width this fires at is pinned by arithmetic rather
   * than taste — the inline form needs 951px minimum, measured — so if this
   * layout ever looks cramped, read the note on the lg block in tokens.css
   * before moving anything.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    /*
     * Drops the nav onto the wordmark's baseline.
     *
     * IT IS AN OPTICAL CONSTANT AND CANNOT BE DERIVED, which is worth stating
     * because CSS looks like it should be able to. Two mechanisms were tried:
     *
     *   - `align-items: baseline` on the header. Lands 44px out. A flex
     *     container's baseline comes from its FIRST flex item, and `.identity`'s
     *     first item is the logo — a replaced element whose baseline is its
     *     bottom margin edge. So the header aligns the nav to the bottom of the
     *     disc rather than to the name.
     *   - Making the wordmark supply that baseline instead, via `align-self:
     *     baseline` on it. That works, but a single baseline participant is
     *     placed at cross-start, so the wordmark jumps 10px up and stops being
     *     centered against the mark.
     *
     * Arithmetic is out too: the offset needs the name's half-leading plus its
     * ASCENT, and CSS exposes no font metric. So it is eye-set, and it measures
     * 0.59px off — better than most derivations would land. Re-check it by eye if
     * either type role or `--chrome-pad-header` moves.
     */
    nav {
      padding-top: var(--space-2);
    }

    /* The inset moves UP to the header, which is what the two `padding-inline: 0`
       declarations below are for. See the note at the top of the file: leaving it
       on the children costs 96px of minimum width and overflows this breakpoint. */
    .masthead {
      display: flex;

      /* Top-aligned so the nav can be dropped onto the wordmark's baseline by
         its own padding above. `flex-start`, NOT `top` — there is no `top` for
         align-items, so that declaration was dropped as invalid and the computed
         value fell back to `normal`, which behaves as `stretch`. It looked right
         because a stretched slot puts its content at the top anyway; this says the
         same thing on purpose, and renders identically. */
      align-items: flex-start;
      justify-content: space-between;
      padding-inline: var(--chrome-inset);
    }
    .name {
      font-size: 1.7578125rem;
    }

    .identity {
      flex-direction: row;
      text-align: start;
      padding-inline: 0;
    }

    /* The band goes away. `padding-block` deliberately stays: the identity is the
       taller child, so it sets the header's height and this padding never shows —
       and it is the breathing room the search field will want in phase 4. */
    .slot {
      background-color: transparent;
      color: var(--color-text);
      padding-inline: 0;
    }

    /* The band is gone, so the underline goes back to the role base.css names —
       and thickens. 3px is the live site's desktop weight; the footer and the
       band both stay on base.css's 2px. Neither is a token: DESIGN.md has
       `border-hairline` at 1px and the quote bar at 4/6, and nothing between. */
    .slot :is(a, button)::after {
      border-block-end-color: var(--color-link-hover);
      border-block-end-width: 3px;
    }

    /* NO RULE UNDER THE MAGNIFIER. Search does not navigate — it replaces this
       slot in place — so borrowing the nav items' hover would say it does. The
       word "Search" on the band keeps the underline, because there it IS reading
       as one of six labels; only the icon-only form drops it.
       Left without a hover treatment on purpose rather than given a new one: the
       theme toggle needs a selected/hover pattern of its own, and reusing that
       once it exists beats inventing a second one here. */
    .search-toggle::after {
      content: none;
    }

    /* Content-width items with a real gap, rather than equal shares of the line.
       `nowrap` because at this width they fit by construction — lg is pinned by
       that arithmetic — so wrapping here would mean the breakpoint is wrong
       rather than that the nav should reflow. */
    ul {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
      column-gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    li {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
    }

    .label-stacked {
      display: none;
    }

    .label-inline {
      display: inline;
    }

    .search-icon {
      display: block;
    }

    /*
     * Visually hidden, NOT removed — the icon is `aria-hidden`, so this text is
     * the button's entire accessible name.
     *
     * NEITHER `visibility: hidden` NOR `width: 0` can do this job:
     *
     *   - `visibility: hidden` takes an element out of the ACCESSIBILITY TREE as
     *     surely as `display: none` does. The button ends up with no accessible
     *     name at all, which is the one thing this rule exists to prevent.
     *   - `width: 0` does not remove text from layout, it only makes it spill.
     *     This label sits at the nav's right edge, so "Search" overflowed 54px
     *     past it — 6px past the viewport — putting a horizontal scrollbar on
     *     every page at this width. Measured: scrollWidth 1206 against 1200.
     *
     * So `overflow: hidden` and the negative margin are load-bearing rather than
     * boilerplate. Every declaration here is doing something.
     *
     * The icon shift this was reaching for is real, and it is fixed on
     * `.search-toggle` instead — see the note there.
     */
    .search-label {
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      margin: -1px;
      padding: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }
</style>
